@titlepage
@title Esploro
@subtitle A file explorer in Emacs, for Lisp desktops
@end titlepage

@node Top
@top Esploro

Esploro (Esperanto for ``exploration'') is a file explorer.  Its window
is a frame of Emacs, built on dired, with what a file manager is
expected to have, for the mouse as much as the keys.  Its core, which
makes every change to files, is Common Lisp, and knows which window
has each file open.

Every change can be undone: copying, moving, pasting, renaming, a new
folder, the Trash and files dropped in are each checked whole before
anything happens, done in the background, and kept in a journal.

@menu
* Opening Esploro::        Super+e, the command, and M-x esploro.
* The window::             The list, the places, the tool bar, the menus.
* Selecting and opening::  Click, double-click, the keys.
* Changing files::         Copy, cut, paste, rename, the Trash, undo.
* Recipes::                The last change, again; or kept by name.
* Drag and drop::          Files in from other programs, and out to them.
* The preview::            The file under the cursor, beside the folder.
* Two panes::              Two folders side by side.
* Windows and workspaces:: One Esploro per workspace, or more.
* Looking around::         Sorting, hidden files, a filter, finding below.
* Searches::               Folders that are questions, kept by name.
* Archives::               A zip or a tarball, opened like a folder.
* The Trash::              Looking in it, restoring, emptying.
* Where files are open::   ``open in Emacs on 2'' beside a file.
* Closing a project::      What's open in it, to save and close.
* Plans from agents::      Changes an agent proposes, for you to apply.
* Keys and mouse::         Everything, on one page.
* The command line::       The esploro command.
* Writing commands::       Commands of your own, in Lisp.
@end menu

@node Opening Esploro
@chapter Opening Esploro

@table @asis
@item @kbd{Super+e}
The Esploro window on the workspace you're on, or a new one there.  On
Vikix this is the key for files; where Esploro isn't installed, it
opens PCManFM instead.

@item @kbd{Super+Alt+Shift+e}
A new Esploro window, even where one is open.

@item @kbd{Super+Alt+r}
Reveals the file behind the window you're in: what an Emacs frame
shows, the document a viewer has open, the file a terminal's program
was started on, or the folder a terminal is in; selected, in its
folder.

@item @code{esploro @var{folder}}
From a shell, the launcher, or a program opening a folder: @var{folder}
in the Esploro window on your workspace, or a new one there.
@code{esploro --new @var{folder}} always opens a new window, and
@code{esploro @var{file}} opens the file's folder with it selected.

@item Show in folder
A browser's ``Show in folder'' (Firefox, Chromium), and any program
that asks for the file manager over D-Bus, opens Esploro at the file's
folder, with the file selected, on your workspace.  On Vikix, folders
open in Esploro too, and so do drives when you plug them in.

@item @kbd{M-x esploro}
From Emacs, once Esploro has been opened (or with @file{emacs/} on
Emacs's @code{load-path}).  With a prefix argument, a new window.
@end table

Esploro's window is a frame of the Emacs that is running, which on
Vikix is a daemon started at login, so it opens at once.  The first
time, the command loads the window's code into that Emacs; nothing in
your Emacs configuration is needed.

To close it, use File, Close Esploro, @kbd{C-x C-c}, or close the window
(@kbd{Super+q} on Vikix).  Only that window closes: Emacs, and its other
frames, go on.

@node The window
@chapter The window

@table @asis
@item The list
The folder's files, folders first, with their size and when they last
changed.  The line at the top says where you are, how the list is
sorted, and when hidden files are shown or a filter is on.

@item Places
Down the left side: Recent (the files you opened lately, newest first:
those you opened through Esploro, and what GTK programs note in
@file{~/.local/share/recently-used.xbel}; also Go @result{} Recent
Files, or @kbd{C-c r}), your home, the usual folders (Documents, Downloads,
Pictures, Music, Videos, Projects, Dropbox, when they exist), drives
plugged in (in @file{/run/media}), the bookmarks GTK programs keep
(@file{~/.config/gtk-3.0/bookmarks}: File @result{} Bookmark This Folder, or
@kbd{C-c b}, adds the folder you're in, and Remove Bookmark takes it
out; PCManFM and the file dialogs share the list), the searches you kept
(@pxref{Searches}), and the Trash.  Click one to go
there.  @kbd{F9}, or View, Places, hides them or shows them again.

@item The tool bar
Back, Forward, Up, Home, Places, Two Panes, Preview, New Folder, Copy,
Cut, Paste, Trash, Undo, Search, Refresh and Help.

@item The menus
Esploro's frame shows only these; your other Emacs frames keep Emacs's
own menus.

@itemize
@item File: new window and folder, open, open with, Commands (what
else suits the file: copy its path, compress, extract, shrink a
picture, yours), properties, a terminal here, closing a project.
@item Edit: undo, repeat the last change and Recipes, copy, cut,
paste and the rest, rename by a pattern, sort into folders by kind,
the Trash, selecting.
@item View: sorting, hidden files, the filter, searching below,
refresh, two panes, the preview, places.
@item Go: back, forward, up, home, a folder typed, your Searches, the
Trash.
@item Help: this manual, and the keys.
@end itemize

Right-click a file for what can be done with it; right-click the
empty part of the list for the folder: paste, a new folder, searching,
sorting.
@end table

@node Selecting and opening
@chapter Selecting and opening

@table @asis
@item Click
Selects the file: the one the next command acts on.

@item @kbd{Ctrl}+click
Adds the file to the selection, or takes it out.

@item @kbd{Shift}+click
Selects every file from the one selected last to the one clicked.

@item Double-click, or @kbd{RET}
Opens it.  A folder opens in the same window.  A file that a window
already has open goes to that window rather than opening a second time.
Text opens in Emacs, in one of its other frames.  Anything else opens
in its usual program (as @file{~/.config/mimeapps.list} says).

@item Right-click
What can be done with the file under the mouse, or, on empty space,
with the folder.

@item File, Open With
Opens the selection with a program you name.
@end table

Edit, Select All, Select None and Invert Selection work on every file
in the folder.  Dired's keys still mark: @kbd{m} marks, @kbd{u}
unmarks, @kbd{U} unmarks all.

@node Changing files
@chapter Changing files

@table @asis
@item Copy, Cut, Paste (@kbd{M-w}, @kbd{C-w}, @kbd{C-y})
Copy or cut the selection, go to another folder, and paste.  Pasting
where the files already are makes copies beside them (``notes
copy.txt'').  Other file managers can paste what Esploro copied, and
Esploro can paste what they copied.

@item Duplicate
A copy of each selected file beside it.

@item Copy To, Move To
Copies or moves the selection into a folder you type.

@item Rename (@kbd{F2})
A new name for the file selected.

@item New Folder (@kbd{+})
A folder in the one you're in.

@item Move to Trash (@kbd{Delete})
Puts the selection in the Trash.  Nothing is deleted for good there.

@item Undo (@kbd{C-/})
Puts back the last change Esploro made.  Undo again for the one before.
Undoing a copy puts the copy in the Trash, rather than deleting it.
@end table

Every change is a plan, a short list of steps, checked whole before
anything happens: a file that would be overwritten, or a folder that
isn't there, stops it with nothing changed, and the message says why.
Changes run in the background, so a long copy doesn't stop Emacs.  Each
one is kept in a journal (@file{~/.local/state/esploro/journal/}),
which is what undo reads.

A long copy (or a move to another disk) says how it goes at the
bottom of the window: the file, how much is done of how much, and
@kbd{C-c C-k} to stop it (Edit @result{} Stop Copying).  Stopped, the
file being copied is taken back, as if it had never started; the files
done before stay, and undo takes them back too.  A move to another disk
is a copy, then the original deleted: stopped during the copy, the
original is as it was; once the delete has begun, it finishes, so a
file is never lost between the two.

Dired's own keys (@kbd{C}, @kbd{R}, @kbd{D} and the rest) still work
in the list, but around the journal: what they do can't be undone from
Esploro.

@node Recipes
@chapter Recipes

Having moved some files into a folder, copied them into one, or put
them in the Trash, select others and press @kbd{z} (Edit @result{}
Repeat Last Change): the same is done to them.  A name already taken
in the folder gets a free one (``report 2.pdf''); a file already there
isn't moved again.

Edit @result{} Recipes @result{} Save Last Change As@dots{} keeps it
by name.  Recipes you kept are listed there, and under Recipes in the
right-click menu, to do on what's selected; Forget a Recipe@dots{} is
there too.  They're kept in
@file{~/.config/esploro/recipes.lisp}.

A recipe is a plan like any other: checked whole, done in the
background, undone with @kbd{C-/}.

@section Habits noticed

Esploro notices what you keep doing.  When three or more like files
(pictures, PDFs, names sharing a word) have gone into one folder, by
two or more of your plans or your corrections to an agent's, it says
so once, at the bottom of the window: ``Esploro noticed: You've moved
3 PDFs and e-books named "invoice" from ~/Downloads into
~/Work/Invoices.''

Edit @result{} Habits Noticed@dots{} lists them, each with:

@table @asis
@item Keep as Rule@dots{}
A sentence for your rules for where files go (@file{sorting.md}), which
agents read: shown first, to say what you mean.
@item Keep as Recipe@dots{}
``Move into that folder'', by name, under Edit @result{} Recipes.
@item Find More
A search for more like them where they came from, to select and move
the same way.
@item Not This
Never offered again.
@end table

Nothing is done on its own.

@section Rename by a pattern

Edit @result{} Rename by Pattern@dots{} (also under Recipes) asks for
the names to change, then what to make of them.  In the first,
@samp{*} stands for any run of characters (as few as will do) and
@samp{?} for any one, in any case; in the second, @samp{#1},
@samp{#2}@dots{} give back what each took, @samp{#n} numbers the files
in the order they're listed (01, 02@dots{}, as wide as the count needs),
and @samp{##} is a @samp{#}.  So @samp{IMG_*.jpg} to
@samp{Holiday #n.jpg} makes ``Holiday 1.jpg'', ``Holiday 2.jpg'' and
so on, and @samp{* - *.mp3} to @samp{#2 - #1.mp3} turns
``Artist - Song.mp3'' round.
Without @samp{*} or @samp{?}, the first is text to replace wherever a
name holds it: a space to @samp{_}.

It takes the marked files, or with none marked everything listed (the
pattern picks its own); names that don't fit are left as they are.

@section Sort into folders by kind

Edit @result{} Sort into Folders by Kind@dots{} sends pictures to
Images, videos to Videos, then Audio, Documents (PDFs, EPUBs), Text
(code too) and Archives: each a folder beside its file, made when it
isn't there.  It too takes the marked files, or everything listed; a
search's files are each sorted in their own folder.  Folders, files of
no kind it names (a spreadsheet) and files already in their folder stay
where they are.  A name already taken in a folder gets a free one
(``notes 2.txt'').

@section Their plans wait for you

Both send each file its own way, so their plan isn't done at once: it
waits under the folder, as an agent's does (@pxref{Plans from agents}).
A rename shows each name before and after; a sorting, how many go
where, what stays and why, and which file got another name.  Apply,
Edit or Cancel; nothing has changed until Apply, and @kbd{C-/} puts it
back after.  A rename that would give two files one name, or a name
that's already taken, isn't offered at all: Esploro says which.

Keep as Recipe@dots{} (@kbd{k}) keeps it by name, under Recipes, to
do again, again for review.  Your own sorting is written in
@file{~/.config/esploro/recipes.lisp}, each kind to a folder: a name
is beside the file, a whole path gathers them there; the first kind a
file is wins, @code{:file} is anything else and @code{:folder} a
folder:

@example
(:recipe "Media" (:sort-by-kind (:image "Pictures") (:video "Pictures/Video")
                                (:pdf "/home/vid/Documents/Papers")))
(:recipe "Holiday" (:rename-by "IMG_*.jpg" "Holiday #n.jpg"))
@end example

@section Duplicates

Edit @result{} Find Duplicates looks below the folder you're in for
files that are the same, byte for byte (a quick hash, a full one, then
compared byte by byte, so nothing is called a copy that isn't).  They
show in groups, the most space wasted first; in each, the oldest says
``kept'' and the others ``copy''.  Edit @result{} Trash the Copies@dots{}
puts the copies in a plan for your review: edit it, apply it, or cancel;
undo puts them back.

Left out: files under 1 KB (a small file twice is usually meant), and
the files of a git repository other than the one you're looking in (the
same LICENSE in two projects is each project's own).

@node Drag and drop
@chapter Drag and drop

@table @asis
@item In
Drag files from another program onto the list: they're copied into the
folder (moved, when the other program says so, often with @kbd{Shift}).
Like every change, it can be undone.  Files dropped on the folder
they're already in are left alone.

@item Out
Press on a file anywhere on its row and drag it to another program: a
browser's upload, a mail, a terminal (which types its name).  It's
copied; hold @kbd{Shift} to move it.  Selected files go together.

@item Between panes
Drag from one pane to the other: the file is copied into that pane's
folder.
@end table

@node The preview
@chapter The preview

Down the right of the window, the file under the cursor:

@table @asis
@item A picture
The picture, scaled to fit.
@item A PDF, a video
The PDF's first page, or a frame of the video.  Esploro makes it once,
in the background (``making a preview...'' while it does), and keeps it
in @file{~/.cache/esploro}, so the next look is at once.
@item Text, code
Its first lines, in the colours its mode gives them.
@item A folder
How many things are in it, and their names.
@item Anything else
What it is, as @command{file} says.
@item Several selected
How many, their size together, and their names.
@end table

Below each: its size, when it last changed, and the windows that have
it open.  The preview follows the cursor and the mouse once they rest,
so holding an arrow key down stays quick.  With two panes, it shows the
pane used last; each Esploro window has its own.

@kbd{F11} (View, Preview, or the tool bar) hides it, or shows it again.
Hidden stays hidden, in every Esploro window, until you turn it on.

@node Two panes
@chapter Two panes

@kbd{F3} (View, Two Panes, or the tool bar) shows two folders side by
side.  The new pane starts at the same folder; each then has its own
folder, history, sort and hidden files.  @kbd{F3} again goes back to
one.

@table @asis
@item Copy to Other Pane (@kbd{C-c C-c})
@itemx Move to Other Pane (@kbd{F6})
The selection, into the other pane's folder.  Both are on the Edit
menu and the right-click menu.
@end table

The places and the tool bar act on the pane used last.

@node Windows and workspaces
@chapter Windows and workspaces

@section Opening on another workspace

Right-click a file (or File) @result{} Open on Workspace lists your
workspaces, each with what it's about: the project its windows are in
(``3   ~/src/vikix''), ``empty'', or how many windows it has.  Pick one
and you're there, with the file open: text in an Emacs frame on that
workspace (one already there, or a new one), a folder in an Esploro
window there, anything else in its usual program.  @kbd{M-o} then the
workspace's number does the same from the keyboard.

@section One Esploro per workspace

Each workspace can have its own Esploro window, at its own folder.
A new one opens in the workspace's project: the folder most of its
windows are about (a terminal's folder, Emacs's files, a viewer's
document), taken up to its project, the nearest folder with a
@file{.git} or a @file{log.md}; your home on an empty workspace.
@kbd{Super+e} goes to the one on the workspace you're on, or opens one
there; one on another workspace stays where it is.  To have two on the
same workspace, use @kbd{Super+Alt+Shift+e}, File, New Window
(@kbd{C-x 5 2}), or @code{esploro --new}.

Esploro knows which window is on which workspace by asking StumpWM.
Without StumpWM, @code{esploro @var{folder}} uses any Esploro window.

@node Looking around
@chapter Looking around

@table @asis
@item Sorting (View, or @kbd{s})
By name, size (largest first), time (newest first) or kind.  The same
again, or View, The Other Way Round, turns it round.  @kbd{s} goes
through them in turn.

@item Hidden files (@kbd{.})
Shows or hides the files whose names start with a dot.

@item Thumbnails (@kbd{T})
A small picture before each picture, PDF and video in the list, made
in the background (a big folder fills in as it goes) and kept, so
they're there at once the next time.  Their size is
@code{esploro-thumbnail-height} (48 pixels).

@item Git
In a folder inside a git repository, each file git has something to say
about says it after its name: @emph{modified}, @emph{new} (git doesn't
follow it yet), @emph{staged} or @emph{added} (for the next commit),
@emph{conflict}; a folder with changes inside says so.  The line at the
top has the branch, and what's waiting: ``git: main, 2 to push''.  Git
is asked in the background each time the folder is shown;
@code{esploro-git-status} set to @code{nil} turns it off.

@item What's Taking Space (@kbd{C-c s})
The folder's files and folders, hidden ones too, by the space they take
on the disk (each folder with all that's in it), biggest first: each
with its size, a bar, and its share.  Go into a folder to see what's
taking its space; Back and Up come out, still measuring.  The Trash,
copying and undo work as anywhere, and F5 measures again.  A big folder
(all of home) takes a while: the top line says ``measuring...'', and
leaving it stops.  @kbd{C-c s} again shows the folder as it was.

The top line says what the folder takes in all, what's free on its
drive, and what the Trash holds when it's on that drive: a file in the
Trash still takes its space, and emptying the Trash is what gives it
back.  Mark files and folders (@kbd{m}) and the top line adds them up,
``marked: 7, 12G (20%)'': what putting them in the Trash, and emptying
it, would free.

@item Biggest Anywhere Below (@kbd{C-c S})
The same view, of the biggest things at any depth below the folder,
each by its way down from it: what takes the space is often four
folders down.  A row is something that takes a hundredth of the folder
or more: a file; a folder whose own things are all smaller, whole (a
cache, a @file{node_modules}); or a folder that holds listed things
too, for ``its smaller things'', the ones beside them.  So no byte is
shown twice, and the rows add up.  At most fifty are shown.  Marking a
folder and something in it counts the folder once, and the Trash takes
the folder alone.  @kbd{C-c S} again shows the folder's own entries;
@kbd{C-c s} the folder as it was.

@item Grid (@kbd{G})
The folder as tiles, side by side: a picture of each picture, PDF and
video, a folder or a page (with its type) for the rest, the name under
it.  Everything works as in the list: click selects (the tile is
outlined), Ctrl+click and Shift+click select more (shaded), a double
click opens, right-click has the same menu, and copy, paste, rename,
the Trash and undo act on the tiles selected.  The arrow keys go from
tile to tile.  Each window remembers whether it's a grid; going into a
folder keeps it.  The size is @code{esploro-grid-size} (128 pixels).

@item Filter (@kbd{/})
Shows only the names holding some text, in any case, until you refresh
(@kbd{F5}).

@item Search Below (@kbd{M-s s})
Every file below this folder that a few words describe, as a list you
can open, copy and drag from like any folder.  Back returns.
@xref{Searches}.

@item Going (@kbd{M-Left}, @kbd{M-Right}, @kbd{M-Up}, @kbd{C-l})
Back, forward, the folder above, and a folder typed, with completion.
@end table

@node Searches
@chapter Searches

View @result{} Search Below@dots{} (@kbd{M-s s}, or right-click the folder) looks below the folder you're
in for the files a few words describe, and shows them as if they were
one folder: open them, drag them, copy or move them as anywhere else.
Every word must hold:

@table @asis
@item @samp{report}
A name holding ``report'', in any case.
@item @samp{*.pdf}, @samp{IMG_20??*}
Names like it: @samp{*} any run of characters, @samp{?} any one.
@item @samp{kind:pdf}
Of a kind: folder, file, image, video, audio, pdf, text, lisp,
archive.
@item @samp{newer:7}, @samp{older:30}
Changed in the last 7 days, or not for 30.
@item @samp{larger:10M}, @samp{smaller:1k}
By size (k, M, G).
@item @samp{has:tender}
A file holding ``tender'' inside it, in any case: text files (found by
@command{ripgrep}, quick), and PDFs (their text taken out once with
@command{pdftotext} and kept, so the first search of many PDFs takes a
while and the next ones don't).
@item @samp{-draft}
Not: a word with @samp{-} in front.
@end table

So @samp{kind:pdf newer:30 -invoice} is the PDFs of the last month that
aren't invoices, and @samp{kind:pdf has:LME} the PDFs that mention LME.  Hidden files and folders aren't looked in, nor what
a project's @file{.gitignore} leaves out (when @command{fd} is
installed), nor @file{node_modules}.  At most 1000 are shown.

@kbd{F5} looks again.  Go @result{} Searches @result{} Keep This
Search@dots{} (or right-click the list) keeps it by name: it's down the side under Searches from then on, a click away,
and always up to date.  They're kept in
@file{~/.config/esploro/searches.lisp}, where a search can also be
written as an s-expression:
@code{(:and (:kind :pdf) (:or (:name "tax") (:name "receipt")))}.

@node Archives
@chapter Archives

Open a zip, a tarball (@file{.tar.gz}, @file{.tgz}, @file{.tar.xz},
@file{.tar.zst}@dots{}), a 7z, a rar or an ISO the way you open a
folder, and you're inside it: the line at the top says ``inside
~/Downloads/pack.zip (read-only)''.  Look around, preview, open files,
copy them out or drag them to another program, as in any folder.

Nothing can be changed inside: copying in, renaming, the Trash are
refused, with what to do instead.  To change what's in it, Commands
@result{} Extract Here, and work on the folder that makes.

Up from its top goes back beside the archive.  Leaving it closes it;
Back goes in again.  It's opened with @command{archivemount}, which
Esploro's feature in Vikix installs; without it, Extract Here still
works.

@node The Trash
@chapter The Trash

Esploro uses the Trash other programs use (the freedesktop one, in
@file{~/.local/share/Trash}).  Go, The Trash (or Trash in the places)
shows what's in it.  There, Restore from the Trash puts the selection
back where it was; undo puts it in the Trash again.  Empty the Trash
deletes everything in it, for good, after asking: that one can't be
undone.  The question says how many things are there and what they
take, which is the space emptying it gives back.

@node Where files are open
@chapter Where files are open

Beside a file that a window has open, Esploro says which: ``open in
Emacs on 2 (unsaved)'', ``open in mpv on 4''.  It asks StumpWM for the
windows and their programs, @file{/proc} for what those hold open or
were started on, and Emacs for its buffers.  Opening such a file goes to
its window instead of opening it again.

@node Closing a project
@chapter Closing a project

Done with a project for now?  File @result{} Close Project@dots{}, in
any folder of it, lists what's open in it: the files not saved first,
each with @samp{Save} and @samp{Close}; then the rest open in Emacs
(files, dired, shells working there), each with @samp{Close}; then the
other windows that have something in it, a terminal in its folder or a
PDF open in a viewer, each with @samp{Go}.

@samp{Save All} saves every unsaved file.  @samp{Close All} closes
what's saved, and leaves what isn't, so nothing is lost by a click:
save it or close it on its own (closing asks first).  Other windows are
for you to close.  @kbd{g} looks again.

A project is the nearest folder above with a @file{.git} or a
@file{log.md} in it.

@node Plans from agents
@chapter Plans from agents

An agent (Claude Code, through Vikix's @code{vikix mcp}) never moves,
copies, renames or deletes your files itself.  It proposes a plan,
which the core checks whole (one with a problem goes back to the agent),
and Esploro shows it to you, on your workspace: why, and each step.
@samp{Apply} makes the changes, journaled, so undo takes them back;
@samp{Cancel} drops the plan, and nothing changes.

@samp{Edit} (or @kbd{e}) opens the plan as text, one step a line:

@example
(:move "/from" "/to")    (:copy "/from" "/to")
(:rename "/path" "new name")
(:mkdir "/path")         (:trash "/path")
@end example

Change, add or delete lines.  Each save checks the plan again, and the
review shows it as it now is, marked as edited by you, with what's
wrong if anything is (a plan with a problem isn't applied).
@kbd{C-c C-c} saves and goes back to the review; @kbd{C-c C-k} drops
your unsaved changes.  Then @samp{Apply} as before.

What you changed is a correction, and Esploro can teach it to every
agent.  After you apply a plan you edited, it says how many of the
agent's steps you changed and asks whether to keep them as rules.  On
yes, each comes as a sentence to make general before it's kept:
``"Dataroom LME.zip" goes in ~/Work, not in ~/Archives.'' might become
``A work zip goes in Work, not in Archives.''  Leave one empty to skip
it.  Kept rules go at the end of your rules for where files go,
@file{~/.config/esploro/sorting.md}, under ``Learnt from my
corrections'', and every agent that proposes changes reads that file
first.  Nothing is written there without your yes; the file is yours to
change (Help @result{} Your Sorting Rules).  Every correction is also
noted, as data, in @file{~/.local/state/esploro/corrections.lisp}.

Agents, and shells, can read what you've selected: @command{esploro
selection} prints the files selected in the Esploro window you used
last (or a dired buffer, if you used one since), one a line; with
nothing marked, the file the cursor is on.  So you can select files,
then ask an agent to ``sort the ones I've selected'', or run
@samp{tar czf out.tgz $(esploro selection)} in a terminal.

